.TH MEMWARE 1 "2026-09-17" "memware 0.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
memware \- local, model\-free memory for AI agents: transcript recall and a bi\-temporal belief ledger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memware
.RB [ \-\-json | \-\-plain ]
.RB [ \-\-ascii ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR FILE ]
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.PP
Global options may appear before or after the
.IR command .
.SH DESCRIPTION
memware is a single SQLite store with two tables. The append\-only
.B turns
index holds past prompts and answers, split into passages and searched with FTS5/BM25. The
bi\-temporal
.B belief
ledger records facts in which a new value for the same
.RI ( subject ", " relation )
.B supersedes
the old one; recall only ever returns the currently valid belief, and history is kept for
audit. There is no daemon, no vector database, and no model call at capture or read time.
.PP
Recall is keyword search; the agent supplies the meaning by passing several phrasings, which
are fused by reciprocal rank.
.PP
Data is written to standard output; diagnostics go to standard error. memware emits no colour
under any circumstances.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create the store.
.TP
.BI "sync " [PATH]
Catch up the configured transcript source (default
.IR ~/.claude/projects )
into the index, or a given
.IR PATH .
.TP
.B backfill
One\-time index of the transcripts already on disk under
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
Idempotent.
.TP
.B exclude
List, add or remove
.B capture.exclude
path globs. A transcript whose resolved path matches one is never indexed by any sync
and never mirrored by
.BR backup ,
and a sync un\-indexes it if it was indexed before. Patterns are matched against the whole
path, so
.B *
crosses
.BR / .
With no flag, prints each pattern with the transcripts on disk and the indexed sources it
matches. Flags:
.BI "\-\-add " GLOB ,
.BI "\-\-remove " GLOB ,
.BR \-\-apply .
A dry run unless
.BR \-\-apply ,
which writes the config and un\-indexes the indexed sources the patterns match.
.TP
.BI "recall " QUERY " ..."
Search turns and beliefs. Pass several phrasings, fused by reciprocal rank. Flags:
.BR \-k " (count),"
.BI "\-\-what " {all,turns,beliefs} ,
.BR \-\-full ", "
.BR \-\-snippet\-tokens .
.TP
.B context
Emit the beliefs whose subject the prompt names, each with the date it was recorded, for
prompt\-time injection. Hook\-friendly. It leaves out what
.B beliefs \-\-stale
lists.
.TP
.B notice
Print what
.B setup
has not asked about yet, one line each, such as whether
.B derive
may run; nothing when there is none. Once per store, it also says how many beliefs injection
leaves out (see
.BR "beliefs \-\-stale" );
the store records that it did, and a store that is not there is never created.
.B \-\-from\-hook
prints Claude Code hook JSON with a
.BR systemMessage ,
which the plugin shows at session start.
.TP
.B digest
Print what memware holds for the project in the current directory: a line pointing at
.BR recall ,
the most recent sessions (date and first prompt) and the beliefs whose subject names the
project, each with the date it was recorded, less what
.B beliefs \-\-stale
lists. A git repository's checkouts and live worktrees count as one project.
Prints nothing for a project with no indexed session. Flags:
.BR \-k ", "
.BR \-\-max\-chars ", "
.BR \-\-cwd ", "
.B \-\-from\-hook
(SessionStart hook JSON).
.TP
.BI "assert " "SUBJECT RELATION VALUE"
Assert a fact; a new value supersedes the prior value for the same key. Flags:
.BR \-\-source ", "
.BR \-\-valid\-from ", "
.BR \-\-reliability ", "
.BR \-\-policy .
Batch mode
.B assert \-
reads
.IR subject <TAB> relation <TAB> value [<TAB> source ]
lines from standard input, skipping blank lines and lines beginning with
.RB \` # \(cq.
.TP
.BI "beliefs " "[SUBJECT [RELATION]]"
Show current beliefs, or the full history of one key. The history keeps retracted beliefs. A
derived belief that was a measurement, a moving version or a status when recorded carries
.BR volatile .
.TP
.B "beliefs \-\-stale"
List the current beliefs the prompt hook and the digest leave out, each with its reason and
why: a derived
.B measurement
(a count or total of rows, tests, files and the like, or a relation that is exactly progress,
coverage or null rate),
.B moving_version
(a version called current, latest, built, installed, deployed, released or on main) or
.B status
(a relation that is exactly status, state or progress, with a status word for a value or an
instance such as a PR, card or run for a subject). Only unambiguous cases count: a qualifier
(slo, target, threshold, budget, commitment, min, max, limit, default, initial, final, required,
every, schedule, check, and the like) always means durable. And, inside a project whose
.IR pyproject.toml ,
.I package.json
or
.I Cargo.toml
declares a version at its root, a belief about a package's version that differs from the one
that package declares, when the subject names it
.RB ( contradicted )
or that names an older version beside the project's name
.RB ( older_version ).
A computed version and a 0.0.0 placeholder are never checked against. A belief a person stated
(reliability above 0.5, or a source that is not a
.I memware:session/
pointer) is never left out, and neither is a derived belief a person confirmed by asserting the
same value again or approving it in review.
.B \-\-cwd
names the project; the default is the current directory. See
.B inject.volatile_days
under
.BR FILES .
.TP
.BI "beliefs retract " "\-\-stale | ID ..."
Retract what
.B beliefs \-\-stale
lists, or the current committed beliefs with these ids; an id naming a superseded belief is
refused, and nothing is written. Nothing is written without
.BR \-\-apply .
Rows are kept, each with its reason; nothing a retracted belief superseded is reopened.
Give ids before any flag.
.TP
.B "beliefs retract \-\-orphaned"
List the committed beliefs whose cited session
.RI ( memware:session/ ID /turn/ N )
is no longer indexed, the predecessors retracting them would reopen, and the beliefs a person
stated that name the session, which are never retracted. Nothing is written without
.BR \-\-apply .
A retracted belief keeps its row.
.TP
.BI "read " SESSION
Read a session's turns back in context. Flags:
.BR \-\-around ", "
.BR \-\-window .
.TP
.B "review list|approve|reject|sync"
Manage contested supersessions through the review channel. A review a prune closed, and a
candidate it redacted or retracted, cannot be approved.
.TP
.B prune
Un\-index matching turns, and retract the beliefs derived from every session left with no
turn. One of
.BR \-\-glob ", "
.B \-\-containing
(whole sources, each transcript file read to the end),
.B \-\-turns\-containing
(turns holding the text anywhere) or
.B \-\-turns\-starting\-with
(turns that begin with it) is required, and a turn selector takes no other selector. Text is
matched literally and case\-sensitively, and never printed or recorded: a retraction's reason
reads
.IR "(value withheld)" .
A text selector written without its text reads it from
.BI \-\-value\-file " FILE" ,
from a prompt that does not echo it, or from standard input; a text of
.B \-
reads standard input. Run a removal from a plain terminal: a command run inside a Claude Code
session is written into that session's transcript. Without
.B \-\-apply
it is a dry run: it prints what it would remove and retract, and the ids of the beliefs it would
redact, and writes nothing. With
.B \-\-apply
every belief whose subject, relation, value or free\-text source holds the text, in any status and
whether or not a person stated it, has the text replaced with
.BR [removed] ,
and a committed one is retracted, and an open review of a redacted belief is closed; a source
memware wrote, such as a session pointer, is never matched or rewritten; no belief row is deleted,
and none is rewritten but to redact the text. An
.B \-\-apply
whose redaction would rewrite more than 20 beliefs, or any belief for a text shorter than 6
characters, is refused whole, writes nothing and exits 2, unless
.BR \-\-allow\-broad\-redaction .
No output prints the text. A selector that
matches nothing says on standard error what it searched and which selector reaches the text
instead. With
.BR \-\-apply ,
a retraction reason an older prune wrote with the text in it is rewritten, and the store file is
scrubbed when anything was removed or copies of the text are left that no row accounts for: both
search indexes are merged (and rebuilt if a term of a deleted row is left), the file is
compacted with
.BR VACUUM ,
and the write\-ahead log is emptied. The file is then checked, and what it still holds is
printed. Standard error names each step and says where copies may remain: backups made before
now, and the transcript files, which memware never changes.
.B \-\-scrub
takes no selector, removes nothing, and rewrites the store file. Exit status is 0 when done, 1
when the store file still holds copies of the text no row accounts for or the scrub did not
finish (it prints the command that finishes it), and 2 on bad usage. Other memware processes
that write wait up to a minute for the store's lock while a prune holds it; recording a recall's
uses waits at most 250 ms and is skipped. A text is one line: trailing line breaks are dropped
and one that still holds a line break is refused.
.TP
.BI "scan " [VALUE]
Count where a value is still stored, read\-only: every transcript under
.B backup.transcript_src
walked on disk, with whether each is indexed and, if not, why
.RB ( capture.exclude ,
the no\-capture list, an ignore marker, or not synced yet); the store file and its write\-ahead
log, as given and in any case, counted before the store is opened read\-only, so no log is ever
checkpointed; the turns, beliefs and other rows holding it; the value's search terms on the index
pages, and those only deleted rows left; and the copies
.B restore
set aside. A store path that is a symlink is followed. With
.B \-\-backups
(or
.BI \-\-dest " DIR" )
it also reads the mirrored transcripts and snapshot files in the backup destination.
.B \-\-transcript\-src
names another tree. A
.I VALUE
left out comes from
.BI \-\-value\-file " FILE" ,
a prompt that does not echo it, or standard input, and
.B \-
reads standard input. Prints paths and counts, never the value or text around it. Exit
status is 0 when nothing is found and every path was read, 1 when anything is found, and 2 when
nothing is found but a path could not be read (each is listed with its reason).
.TP
.B stats
Print store counts; sessions, turns and current beliefs by the entrypoint that started the
session, and the five project directories holding the most sessions; derive state (sources,
runs, last run, turns not yet derived) and recall
utilization (beliefs and turns recalled in 7 and 30 days, beliefs citing a session that is no
longer indexed, and beliefs citing a turn id that no longer exists though the session is still
indexed \(em a stale citation left by re\-indexing, distinct from a session that is fully
gone); the beliefs injection leaves out, by reason, for the project in the current directory;
with a one\-line verdict when memory is inert, such as an empty ledger because derive has
never run, or when beliefs have lost their session.
.TP
.B backup
Write a tiered snapshot (1/3/7/14\-day) plus a transcript mirror. Flags:
.BR \-\-dest ", "
.BR \-\-keep ", "
.BR \-\-if\-stale ", "
.BR \-\-quiet .
.TP
.B restore
Restore the store from a snapshot. Flags:
.BR \-\-from ", "
.BR \-\-latest .
.TP
.B setup
Guided one\-time setup: index existing sessions, pick a backup destination, take a first backup,
and ask whether to switch on automatic
.BR derive .
.TP
.BI "config " "KEY [VALUE]"
Get or set a configuration value.
.TP
.B nuke
Permanently delete all memware data.
.B \-\-confirm
requires typing the exact phrase
.RB \` "DELETE ALL MEMWARE DATA" \(cq.
.TP
.B "completions {bash,zsh,fish}"
Print a shell completion script (generated with
.IR shtab ,
which requires the
.B [shell]
extra). Exits 2 if
.I shtab
is not installed.
.SH OPTIONS
Global flags, valid before or after the command:
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable JSON output.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Tab\-separated records, one per line, id\-first, in a fixed column order; tabs and newlines in
values collapse to spaces. For piping to
.BR fzf ", " awk ", " cut .
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
ASCII only \(em replaces the Unicode horizontal ellipsis elision glyph (U+2026) with three
ASCII dots
.RB (\` ... \(cq).
Also switches on automatically when the locale is not UTF\-8.
.TP
.BI "\-\-db " FILE
Store path (environment
.BR MEMWARE_DB ).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
The default human output (no
.BR \-\-json / \-\-plain )
for the record listers
.RB ( recall ", " beliefs ", " read )
is labeled: one
.RI \` "field : value" \(cq
per line, a blank line between records, empty fields skipped.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MEMWARE_DB
Store path (same as
.BR \-\-db ).
.TP
.B MEMWARE_HOME
Config and store directory. XDG\-aware: uses
.B MEMWARE_HOME
if set, else
.I ~/.memware
if it already exists, else
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/memware ,
else
.IR ~/.memware .
.TP
.B MEMWARE_ASCII
Set to
.B 1
for ASCII output (same as
.BR \-\-ascii ).
.TP
.B MEMWARE_NO_CAPTURE
Set to
.B 1
in a session's environment to keep it out of the index and the backup mirror. Each memware hook
that runs in the session adds its transcript to
.IR <home>/no-capture.txt ,
which every sync and backup honours; a session that runs no memware hook cannot be recognised.
.TP
.B MEMWARE_IGNORE_MARKERS
Content signatures, separated by newlines or
.BR : ;
a transcript whose head contains one is never indexed or mirrored.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Honoured by construction \(em memware emits no colour at all.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <home>/config.json
Configuration, including the
.B capture.exclude
path globs (see
.BR exclude )
and
.BR inject.volatile_days :
a derived measurement, moving version or status is injected while younger than this many days.
The default, 0, never injects one;
.B config
refuses a value that is not a number of days, 0 or more.
.TP
.I <home>/ignore-markers.txt
Content signatures for transcripts that must never be indexed or mirrored.
.TP
.I <home>/no-capture.txt
Transcripts of sessions run under
.BR MEMWARE_NO_CAPTURE ,
one path per line; never indexed or mirrored.
.PP
.I <home>
is resolved as described for
.B MEMWARE_HOME
above.
.SH ACCESSIBILITY
memware never conveys information by colour and emits no colour escapes. Default output is
labeled, one field per line, for screen readers;
.B \-\-plain
and
.B \-\-json
are stable machine formats. Data goes to stdout and diagnostics to stderr.
.B \-\-ascii
(or a non\-UTF\-8 locale) avoids non\-ASCII glyphs. See
.I docs/accessibility.md
for the full statement.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (for example,
.B assert
missing
.I VALUE
without
.BR \- ,
or
.B completions
when
.I shtab
is not installed).
.SH EXAMPLES
Catch up new transcripts into the index:
.PP
.RS
.EX
memware sync ~/.claude/projects
.EE
.RE
.PP
Assert a fact, superseding any prior value:
.PP
.RS
.EX
memware assert "api" "listens on port" "8443" \-\-source "session 3f2a, turn 41"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Recall a belief with several phrasings:
.PP
.RS
.EX
memware recall "which port does the api use" "api port" "8443" \-\-what beliefs
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pick a hit with fzf and read its session back:
.PP
.RS
.EX
memware recall "gateway port" \-\-plain | fzf \-\-delimiter='\et' \-\-with\-nth=10 \e
  | cut \-f4 | xargs \-r memware read
.EE
.RE
.SH INSTALLATION
Read this page without installing it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
man \-l docs/memware.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Or install it by copying it into a
.I man1
directory on your
.BR MANPATH ,
for example
.IR ~/.local/share/man/man1/memware.1 ,
then run
.BR "man memware" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR docs/design.md ,
.IR docs/backup.md ,
.IR docs/integrations.md ,
.IR docs/keeping-memory-clean.md ,
.IR docs/editor-integration.md ,
.IR docs/accessibility.md .
.PP
